      <div class="col-lg-9 guide-body">
        <h2>Why Utah spa owners look at Covana</h2>
        <p>A hot tub cover only saves energy if it actually goes back on. On a snowy January night in the Salt Lake Valley, wrestling a heavy, wet foam cover is the chore many owners put off. A Covana opens and closes with a key, so the spa stays covered between soaks and holds its heat.</p>
        <p>Open, the same cover becomes a gazebo-style shelter that keeps falling snow, wind and summer sun off you while you soak, with privacy screens in several opacity levels. Closed, the airtight seal cuts heat loss and evaporation and keeps leaves, debris and snow out of the water. The sloped roofs on the Oasis and Oasis Midnight shed snow naturally.</p>
        <p>Pick the model by your spa's shape: <strong>Oasis</strong> or <strong>Oasis Midnight</strong> for most square and rectangular hot tubs, <strong>Evolution</strong> for round, square or rectangular spas up to 135 by 92 inches, and <strong>Legend</strong> for <a href="/spa-models/swim-spas/">swim spas</a> up to 241 inches long. If you're also weighing a standard cover and lifter, compare options on our <a href="/covers/">covers page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

